# Git hygiene

## Commits

- One logical change per commit. Do not bundle unrelated changes.
- Write commit messages that explain *why*, not just *what*. The diff shows what changed; the message explains the intent.
- Use the imperative mood in the subject line (e.g. "Add user validation" not "Added user validation").
- Keep the subject line under 72 characters. Add a body for non-trivial changes.

## Branches

- Create a branch for any work that spans more than one commit.
- Use descriptive branch names: `feature/<slug>`, `fix/<slug>`, `chore/<slug>`.
- Keep branches short-lived. Merge or rebase frequently to avoid drift.

## Pull requests

- Every PR should be reviewable in isolation — it tells a complete story.
- Reference the relevant plan (`docs/plans/`) or feature doc (`docs/features/`) in the PR description.
- Do not merge without passing verification (tests, type-check, lint).

## What not to commit

- Secrets, credentials, API keys, or tokens — even temporarily.
- Generated artifacts (build outputs, lock files from other package managers) unless the project ADR specifies otherwise.
- Large binary files without a clear reason and an entry in `.gitignore` for similar future files.
